# English numbers and English units

When a value uses Western digits (`0-9`), its unit must stay in **English** — even in Arabic UI. Surrounding sentence text can be Arabic.

## Always use

| Type | Format | Arabic UI example |
|------|--------|-------------------|
| Money | `49 SAR`, `134 SAR/mo`, `15,219 SAR/yr` | `ادفع 49 SAR` |
| File size | `1.8 MB`, `245.3 KB`, `25 MB` | `الحد الاقصى 25 MB` |
| Percent | `32%` | `32% من المجموع` |
| Count + English noun | `6 files`, `90 sec` | OK when the noun is English |

## Never use

- Arabic-Indic digits (`٠-٩`) in UI copy
- Arabic currency next to Western digits (`49 ريال`, `١٣٤ ريال/شهر`)
- Transliterated units (`ميقا`, `كيلو`)
- `ar-SA` number formatting for display (`toLocaleString("ar-SA")`)
- Arabic thousands separators with Western digits (`١٥٬٢١٩`)

## Implementation

- Use helpers from `src/lib/format.ts`: `formatInt`, `formatSar`, `formatBytes`, `formatByteLimit`, `PRICE_LABEL`
- Wrap `number + unit` in `ltr-always` or `dir="ltr"` inside RTL layouts when needed
- Parsers may normalize Arabic digits from bank files; display must still use Western digits

## Examples

- Bad: `الحد الاقصى ٢٥ ميقا`
- Good: `الحد الاقصى 25 MB`

- Bad: `ادفع ٤٩ ريال`
- Good: `ادفع 49 SAR`

- Bad: `1.8 MB من 25 ميقا`
- Good: `6 ملفات (1.8 MB / 25 MB)`
